From: Adriel Santos <adriel@adriel.eu>
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: ath11k: nss: cap the Tx queue per radio below the payload pool

NSS gives every radio 8192 Tx descriptors, plus a 1024-packet queue for
packets waiting for one. Each packet held there pins an NSS payload
buffer until its Tx completion, and the same pool refills every Rx ring.
The RD03v2 caps that pool at 4096 (nss-bufpool), with ~2300 of them in
the Rx rings at idle, so one radio's Tx side can hold more buffers than
the whole pool.

When a client receives slower than another sends to it, TCP fills the
AP's queue to that client. On the bench, a VHT80 station sending to an
HT20 station on the same 5 GHz AP (iperf3, 2 streams, 30 s):

  - The queue to the slow client grew to ~2600 packets.
  - Free payloads went to 0 (+630k to +750k allocation failures).
  - The 5 GHz Rx ring drained to ~170 of 1023.
  - The radio's datapath froze for ~3.5 s at a time: 3-7 s of 0 bit/s
    per run, the stalls reported in issue #18.

A slow 2.4 GHz client drains the same shared pool and starves 5 GHz Rx.
Raising the pool to 8704 only moves the threshold: with 8 streams the
queue reached 6410 and the stalls came back.

Cap the descriptors at 1024 per radio by default, and the waiting queue
at a quarter of that (256). A full queue then drops at enqueue, which
TCP handles as ordinary loss, and the Rx rings keep their buffers.
Same image and bench, same test, old values vs new:

  - 0 bit/s:          3-6 s        -> none
  - retransmits:      700-3300     -> 120-200
  - free payloads:    min 0        -> min 560
  - alloc failures:   +630k/+750k  -> 0
  - throughput:       34 Mbit/s    -> 48 Mbit/s

8 streams, cross-band, and both radios feeding slow clients at once had
no stalls either. Throughput to fast clients is unchanged (172-360
Mbit/s, as on v1.10), and firmware-crash recovery still works on both
radios. The descriptor memory drops from ~2.2 MB to ~0.4 MB per radio:
idle MemAvailable +3.6 MB.

The nss_tx_desc module parameter (256-8192) sets another size; 8192
restores the old descriptor count and queue.
---
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/nss.c
@@ -4267,6 +4267,22 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/* How many packets NSS may hold in one radio's Tx path. Each one pins an
+ * NSS payload buffer until its Tx completion, and the same buffers refill
+ * every Rx ring. The donor's 8192 per radio is twice the RD03v2's whole
+ * pool (n2h_empty_pool_buf_core0 = 4096, ~2300 of them sitting in the Rx
+ * rings), so the queue to one slow client could take every free buffer:
+ * Rx refill then fails, the radio's datapath freezes for seconds, and TCP
+ * falls into retransmission timeouts (issue #18). With the cap, a full
+ * queue drops at enqueue instead, which TCP treats as ordinary loss.
+ */
+#define ATH11K_NSS_TX_DESC_MIN		256
+#define ATH11K_NSS_TX_DESC_DEFAULT	1024
+
+static unsigned int ath11k_nss_tx_desc = ATH11K_NSS_TX_DESC_DEFAULT;
+module_param_named(nss_tx_desc, ath11k_nss_tx_desc, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(nss_tx_desc, "NSS Tx descriptors per radio, 256-8192 (default 1024)");
+
 static int ath11k_nss_fill_tx_desc_info(struct ath11k_base *ab,
 					struct nss_wifili_init_msg *wim)
 {
@@ -4277,10 +4293,21 @@
 	u32 tx_desc_limit_1 = 0;
 	u32 tx_desc_limit_2 = 0;
 	u32 dam_page_idx = 0;
+	u32 num_tx_desc;
 	int page_idx = 0;
 	int i;
 
-	wim->tx_sw_internode_queue_size = ATH11K_WIFIILI_MAX_TX_PROCESSQ;
+	num_tx_desc = clamp_t(u32, ath11k_nss_tx_desc, ATH11K_NSS_TX_DESC_MIN,
+			      ATH11K_WIFILI_DBDC_NUM_TX_DESC);
+	if (num_tx_desc != ath11k_nss_tx_desc)
+		ath11k_warn(ab, "nss_tx_desc %u out of range, using %u\n",
+			    ath11k_nss_tx_desc, num_tx_desc);
+
+	/* Packets waiting for a free descriptor also pin payload buffers;
+	 * scale that queue down with the descriptors (8192 keeps 1024).
+	 */
+	wim->tx_sw_internode_queue_size = min_t(u32, ATH11K_WIFIILI_MAX_TX_PROCESSQ,
+						num_tx_desc / 4);
 
 	dim = &wim->wtdim;
 	dam = &wim->wtdam;
@@ -4291,16 +4318,16 @@
 	//TODO Revisit below calc based on platform/mem cfg
 	switch (dim->num_pool) {
 	case 1:
-		tx_desc_limit_0 = ATH11K_WIFILI_DBDC_NUM_TX_DESC;
+		tx_desc_limit_0 = num_tx_desc;
 		break;
 	case 2:
-		tx_desc_limit_0 = ATH11K_WIFILI_DBDC_NUM_TX_DESC;
-		tx_desc_limit_1 = ATH11K_WIFILI_DBDC_NUM_TX_DESC;
+		tx_desc_limit_0 = num_tx_desc;
+		tx_desc_limit_1 = num_tx_desc;
 		break;
 	case 3:
-		tx_desc_limit_0 = ATH11K_WIFILI_DBTC_NUM_TX_DESC;
-		tx_desc_limit_1 = ATH11K_WIFILI_DBTC_NUM_TX_DESC;
-		tx_desc_limit_2 = ATH11K_WIFILI_DBTC_NUM_TX_DESC;
+		tx_desc_limit_0 = num_tx_desc;
+		tx_desc_limit_1 = num_tx_desc;
+		tx_desc_limit_2 = num_tx_desc;
 		break;
 	default:
 		ath11k_warn(ab, "unexpected num radios during tx desc alloc\n");
